WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:05.430
They say that Hollywood is a bulk business,
and 2023 is not shaping up to be an exception.

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:09.090
We're only halfway through the year, and there
are already some truly awful movies out there.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:11.311
These are the worst of the worst.

00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:14.210
The world is still trying to make sense of
the COVID-19 pandemic.

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:18.600
One of the purposes of art is to unpack the
emotions, lessons, and impact of shared human

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:19.810
experiences like this.

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:25.006
It's unsurprising, then, that a curious sub-genre
of pandemic movies has developed in recent years.

00:00:25.006 --> 00:00:29.630
By and large, these somber, anxious, and surreal
movies have been met with savage critical assessments.

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:33.850
Perhaps not enough time has passed to gain
an interesting artistic perspective, or maybe

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:37.057
nobody wants to relive the most painful days
of 2020.

00:00:37.057 --> 00:00:41.239
Either way, movies like "Locked Down" and
"The Bubble" were not well received, and in

00:00:41.239 --> 00:00:43.450
2023, "Life Upside-Down" joined them.

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:48.341
"Life Upside Down" consists of intertwining
stories about a few hard-to-root-for Los Angeles couples.

00:00:48.341 --> 00:00:52.699
Art gallery owner Jonathan is stuck quarantining
with his wife, but he misses his mistress,

00:00:52.699 --> 00:00:56.219
Clarissa, who lives solo and interacts with
the world at a distance.

00:00:56.219 --> 00:01:00.670
Sheltering at home makes everyone sad, mad,
and upset, feelings that audiences will certainly

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:03.402
remember experiencing themselves not that
long ago.

00:01:03.402 --> 00:01:04.971
This did not land well.

00:01:04.971 --> 00:01:08.080
Nell Minow of RogerEbert.com had this to say:

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:12.150
"The characters are shallow and tiresomely
needy, not to make any point or learn any

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.759
lessons, just due to a lack of imagination."

00:01:14.759 --> 00:01:16.963
Minow was far from alone in this opinion.

00:01:16.963 --> 00:01:19.563
Critics absolutely lambasted the film.

00:01:19.563 --> 00:01:23.490
Released in 1990, the original "House Party"
is a modern comedy classic.

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:28.150
The light-hearted, likable, and low-key ensemble
film starred then-popular rap duo Kid N' Play

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:31.950
as a couple of high school friends who pull
off an epic rager despite numerous setbacks

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:34.194
that threaten to derail things at every turn.

00:01:34.194 --> 00:01:37.614
It spawned a couple of okay sequels, and,
in 2023, a remake.

00:01:37.614 --> 00:01:41.790
The new film ups the stakes, the wacky comedy,
and the celebrity branding, which results

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:43.361
in an incongruous jumble.

00:01:43.361 --> 00:01:47.656
This time, the partiers are adults, best friends,
and failed promoters, Damon and Kevin.

00:01:47.656 --> 00:01:51.759
About to lose their low-paying jobs as house
cleaners and on the verge of financial ruin,

00:01:51.759 --> 00:01:55.189
they decide to throw a shindig in the last
house they're scheduled to clean: A mansion

00:01:55.189 --> 00:01:57.822
belonging to NBA superstar LeBron James.

00:01:57.822 --> 00:02:02.010
The rest of the movie consists of material
celebrating and lightly teasing James, drug-fueled

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:04.305
party hijinks, and rapper cameos.

00:02:04.305 --> 00:02:07.820
Critics didn't outright loathe the new "House
Party" across the board, but they were largely

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:11.078
unimpressed and confused as to why it needed
to exist.

00:02:11.078 --> 00:02:13.607
As Richard Roeper of "The Chicago Sun-Times"
put it,

00:02:13.607 --> 00:02:17.739
"The bash at LeBron James' mansion ends up
feeling more exhausting than exciting."

00:02:17.739 --> 00:02:20.129
Brian Richards of "Pajiba" agreed, writing,

00:02:20.129 --> 00:02:23.840
"This version wasn't as funny or as good as
it could've and should've been, and it doesn't

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:26.709
even have the same level of heart as the original."

00:02:26.709 --> 00:02:29.950
Reinventing the romantic comedy is a noble
and ambitious effort.

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:34.277
Attempting to explore a love story through
the lens of modern technology is even more enticing.

00:02:34.277 --> 00:02:37.800
Millions of people spend their whole lives
on sophisticated smartphones, but it's hard

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:41.020
to make staring at screens, smiling, particularly
romantic.

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:45.010
That's the trouble with "Your Place or Mine,"
a Netflix original starring the usually charming

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:49.498
Reese Witherspoon and Aston Kutcher, both
of whom have extensive rom-com experience.

00:02:49.498 --> 00:02:52.553
Their characters, Debbie and Peter, had a
fling 20 years ago.

00:02:52.553 --> 00:02:55.315
They're now best friends who live on opposite
coasts.

00:02:55.315 --> 00:02:59.239
After they get a taste of each other's lives
by switching houses, she goes to Los Angeles,

00:02:59.239 --> 00:03:02.405
he regroups in New York, they wind up falling
in love.

00:03:02.405 --> 00:03:05.846
Almost the entirety of their relationship
unfolds via phone calls.

00:03:05.846 --> 00:03:10.629
"Your Place or Mine" couldn't quite overcome
its own restrictions, earning a critical shellacking.

00:03:10.629 --> 00:03:12.210
Rex Reed of the "Observer" wrote,

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:14.739
"About as romantic and funny as a root canal."

00:03:14.739 --> 00:03:19.199
"Is it safe…?"

00:03:22.118 --> 00:03:26.640
Others agreed, calling the movie a flat exercise
in rom-com tropes cutting-edge technology

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:27.670
can't save.

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:32.510
In 1978, horror master Stephen King published
the short story "Children of the Corn" in

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:34.069
the anthology "Night Shift."

00:03:34.069 --> 00:03:38.400
Before long, Hollywood adapted the creepy
tale of a tiny Midwestern farm town populated

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:40.769
entirely by sacrifice-obsessed children.

00:03:40.769 --> 00:03:45.370
After making the first "Children of the Corn"
movie in 1984, the filmmakers never stopped.

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:48.730
The latest "Children of the Corn" is the 11th
entry in the franchise.

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:50.879
This time, the story unfolds a little differently.

00:03:50.879 --> 00:03:54.680
The terrifying cult leader is a 12-year-old
girl instead of a boy, and it's a plucky high

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:58.890
school student who dares to stand up to her
and her army of adult-killing kids.

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:03.889
This latest take on the story has additionally
differentiated itself by earning truly execrable reviews.

00:04:03.889 --> 00:04:06.346
Richard Whittaker of "The Austin Chronicle"
said,

00:04:06.346 --> 00:04:08.908
"It's all so ridiculous and implausible."

00:04:08.908 --> 00:04:11.800
Sara Michelle Fetters of "Moviefreak" concurred,
writing,

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:15.886
"The only emotional response this 'Children
of the Corn' scares up is boredom."

00:04:15.886 --> 00:04:19.560
A daringly sinister slaughter of a sacred
cow, "Winnie-the-Pooh: Blood and Honey" boasts

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:21.949
a simple premise: It's a horror version of
A.A.

00:04:21.949 --> 00:04:26.210
Milne's classic stories of the Hundred Acre
Wood, famously populated by Pooh, Piglet,

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:28.870
Tigger, Eeyore, and their human friend Christopher
Robin.

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:33.436
The work entered the public domain in 2022,
so it's fair game for interpretation.

00:04:33.436 --> 00:04:36.949
"Winnie-the-Pooh: Blood and Honey" doesn't
do much to analyze or satirize its well-known

00:04:36.949 --> 00:04:38.550
source material, however.

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:42.430
The movie is a by-the-numbers slasher dressed
in Pooh's clothing, and that novelty wears

00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:43.430
off quickly.

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:46.889
The plot involves little more than murderous
incarnations of Pooh and Piglet attacking

00:04:46.889 --> 00:04:50.479
Christopher Robin and several young women
staying in a nearby cabin.

00:04:50.479 --> 00:04:52.707
Kyle Turner of "The New York Times" laments,

00:04:52.707 --> 00:04:56.370
"It's not funny enough to have anything clever
to say about its gag, and it's not exciting

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:58.520
enough to be a competent horror movie."

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:02.539
It's as if the makers of "Paint" set out to
make a biopic about big-haired public television

00:05:02.539 --> 00:05:06.210
art instructor Bob Ross but then couldn't
get the rights, so they decided to make a

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:08.949
different movie about a guy who just looks
like Bob Ross.

00:05:08.949 --> 00:05:12.979
"Paint," written and directed by Brit McAdams,
best known for his work behind the camera

00:05:12.979 --> 00:05:17.831
on Comedy Central's "Tosh.0," seems to want
to make fun of Bob Ross for no discernible reason.

00:05:17.831 --> 00:05:22.360
The usually charming and effortlessly funny
Owen Wilson stars in "Paint" as Carl Nargle.

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:26.040
He's a man very satisfied with his life and
appreciative of it too.

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:30.009
Carl has everything an enlightened man of
the 1970s could want, like his own painting

00:05:30.009 --> 00:05:34.790
show on Vermont public TV, adoring fans who
paint along with him at home, and laudable hair.

00:05:34.790 --> 00:05:39.270
He then falls into an ugly but predictable
movie-style existential crisis when another,

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:43.479
younger, better painter comes along, threatens
his comfortable existence, and makes him confront

00:05:43.479 --> 00:05:45.340
some ugly truths.

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:49.539
To compete in a crowded marketplace of special
effects-laden, intricately-plotted, and would-be

00:05:49.539 --> 00:05:54.110
blockbusters, action and sci-fi filmmakers
have to lay out as many new ideas as possible.

00:05:54.110 --> 00:05:58.620
But "65," written and directed by Scott Beck
and Bryan Woods, the team responsible for

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:03.169
the screenplay for "A Quiet Place," went simple,
assembling recognizable tropes from well-known

00:06:03.169 --> 00:06:04.550
sci-fi properties.

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:07.444
The action begins on the futuristic planet
of Somaris.

00:06:07.444 --> 00:06:11.680
A pilot named Mills volunteers for a two-year
space mission to get the money for the miracle

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:13.429
cure for his daughter's illness.

00:06:13.429 --> 00:06:17.539
But he gets lost in space and crash-lands
on an unfamiliar planet, along with a child

00:06:17.539 --> 00:06:18.539
named Koa.

00:06:18.539 --> 00:06:22.210
It's no big surprise to the audience when
the planet turns out to be Earth, but it's

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:25.810
65 million years in the past, hence the generic
title of the movie.

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:30.000
Do the two run for their lives from giant
dinosaurs and then also falling space rocks?

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:30.897
Of course they do.

00:06:30.897 --> 00:06:35.060
Unfortunately, the end result isn't any fun,
and it doesn't feel particularly inspired.

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:38.580
"Your scientists were so preoccupied with
whether or not they could, then didn't stop

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:39.432
to ask if they should."

00:06:39.432 --> 00:06:42.371
You've seen this movie before with different
names and different characters.

00:06:42.371 --> 00:06:46.930
On Rotten Tomatoes, "65" cobbled together
a mediocre 35% critical score.

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:49.180
Rex Reed of "The Observer" savaged it saying,

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:54.244
"'65' is a gruesome thing to watch, even for
dinosaur lovers — and not much fun, either."

00:06:54.244 --> 00:06:59.186
Actors are supposed to be performance chameleons,
disappearing into their roles to tell a compelling story.

00:06:59.186 --> 00:07:03.180
"Ghosted" stars two actors so famous, radiant,
and associated with other roles that it's

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:07.080
nearly impossible to believe them in this
romance-action-comedy that cribs from so many

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:07.969
other movies.

00:07:07.969 --> 00:07:12.080
Chris Evans, best known for his multiple outings
as Captain America, unconvincingly plays a

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:13.639
regular guy named Cole.

00:07:13.639 --> 00:07:17.540
He goes on a date with a mysterious and captivating
woman named Sadie, who's so secretive that

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:18.392
it's off-putting.

00:07:18.392 --> 00:07:20.270
It's almost as if she's a spy.

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:23.610
She is a spy, of course, and as their young
relationship develops, they fall into the

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:25.800
midst of a dangerous international caper.

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:29.419
Sadie sternly and violently fights the couple's
way out of trouble while Cole can't believe

00:07:29.419 --> 00:07:33.577
the craziness around him, even after wild
things keep happening every five minutes.

00:07:33.577 --> 00:07:38.227
As a result, "Ghosted" is essentially a greatest-hits
collection of familiar action movie tropes.

00:07:38.227 --> 00:07:42.740
With a 26% rating on Rotten Tomatoes, "Ghosted"
is a frothy frivolity that didn't leave a

00:07:42.740 --> 00:07:44.360
good impression on critics.

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:47.230
Wenlei Ma wrote on Australia's "News.com,"

00:07:47.230 --> 00:07:50.319
"This was the leftover scraps cobbled together
from better scripts."

00:07:50.319 --> 00:07:54.819
Clarisse Loughrey of The Independent accused
"Ghosted" of "trying to brainwash its audience"

00:07:54.819 --> 00:07:57.667
on account of the lack of spark between the
leads.

00:07:57.667 --> 00:08:01.979
"Love Again" is a movie out of its time, an
earnest but ultimately dull and dated throwback

00:08:01.979 --> 00:08:03.729
to '90s romantic comedy movies.

00:08:03.729 --> 00:08:07.180
But it's got a little something extra in the
form of grief and sadness, which the film

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:09.000
can't quite ever overcome.

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:13.620
That makes for a tonally weird movie, flitting
from Priyanka Chopra Jonas' Mira mourning

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:17.490
the death of her fiancé to Mira going on
a wacky date with a doofus played by Chopra

00:08:17.490 --> 00:08:22.248
Jonas' husband, Nick Jonas, a prolonged wink
to the presumably in-the-know audience.

00:08:22.248 --> 00:08:24.960
The plot kicks off with a heartbreaker of
an inciting incident.

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:29.310
Mira sends lovey-dovey text messages to her
deceased fiancé's old phone number, unaware

00:08:29.310 --> 00:08:33.209
it's been given to Rob, a journalist who just
so happens to be an attractive man roughly

00:08:33.209 --> 00:08:34.187
Mira's age.

00:08:34.187 --> 00:08:38.800
At the moment, he's spending an inordinately
long time profiling Celine Dion, presented

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:40.779
as a contemporary mega-star.

00:08:40.779 --> 00:08:45.610
As you might guess based on that bit of info,
"Love Again" leans hard into the '90s vibes.

00:08:45.610 --> 00:08:50.664
Celine believes in love, and so she plays
Cupid for Rob and Mira.

00:08:50.664 --> 00:08:52.023
"You okay?"

00:08:52.023 --> 00:08:56.000
Kate: "I, uh, just threw up in my mouth a
little bit"

00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:01.110
A 24% score makes "Love Again" a Rotten Tomatoes
dud, with critics decidedly not charmed by

00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:01.610
the movie.

00:09:01.610 --> 00:09:03.938
David Ehrlich of "IndieWire" bemoaned,

00:09:03.938 --> 00:09:07.240
"Most of the laugh lines in 'Love Again' are
stale enough that even just hearing them kind

00:09:07.240 --> 00:09:08.290
of hurts your teeth."

00:09:08.290 --> 00:09:12.339
At least two critics had pretty much the same
complaint, with Marah Eakin of the Chicago

00:09:12.339 --> 00:09:16.390
Reader saying she didn't enjoy the movie's
"Hallmark-level plot" and Rex Reed of The

00:09:16.390 --> 00:09:19.642
Observer negatively comparing the whole thing
to "a Hallmark card."

